Some connect this verse with the foregoing, as a special reason why their eyes were darkened, — 17. For this become faint did our heart;
For these things darkened were our eyes, 18. Yea, for mount Sion, which is desolate;
Foxes have walked through it. “This” was the “woe” which sin had brought; and “these things” were the various things which he had previously stated, but the desolation of mount Sion was the chief cause of sorrow. Others take this verse by itself, as it is done by the Sept., where על is rendered “on,” and ש for אשר, is translated “because,” — On mount Sion, because it has become desolate,
Foxes have walked in (or through) it. If על be rendered concerning, or, as to, or, with regard to, the best construction would be the following, — As to mount Sion, which has become desolate,
Foxes have walked in it (or, traversed it.) — Ed
